 from 2004Q1 to 2025Q4 using a prespecified case-non-case design. Tier A comprised direct high-certainty non-PML neuroinvasive infection terms. Tier B comprised a possible CNS infectious syndrome plus pathogen evidence elsewhere in the report, without CNS confirmation; tier A+B was a lower-certainty expanded endpoint for sensitivity and pathogen composition analyses. We estimated overall reporting (E1), pathogen composition within tier A+B (E2), and same-pathogen CNS localization (E3). Five DMT mechanisms were compared with contemporaneous active DMTs using calendar-year Mantel-Haenszel reporting odds ratios and covariate-adjusted Jeffreys-prior penalized logistic models. Results Among 492,514 reports, 675 were tier A and 82 additional reports were tier B. Tier A aRORs (95% CIs) were…"}
{"hit_count":4439}"}, {"id": "PMID:42991313", "text": "{"id":"PMID:42991313","title":"Is it time for anti-CD20 discontinuation in multiple sclerosis? Cumulative risk or cumulative uncertainty.","year":"2026","journal":"Frontiers in immunology","abstract":"The widespread adoption of anti-CD20 monoclonal antibodies (anti-CD20 mAbs) in multiple sclerosis (MS) has prompted increasing interest in treatment discontinuation and de-escalation strategies. In this perspective, we critically examine current data on outcomes following anti-CD20 discontinuation, including evidence from registries, prospective cohorts, and real-world studies. Available findings suggest that disease control achieved under anti-CD20 therapy is pharmacologically maintained rather than biologically resolved, and that B-cell repopulation following treatment cessation is heterogeneous and not reliably predictive of clinical stability. Reports of disease reactivation after discontinuation and the absence of validated biomarkers to identify patients who may safely stop therapy highlight substantial uncertainty. Current evidence therefore remains insufficient to support routine anti-CD20 discontinuation in MS, while a cautious, individualized approach-potentially incorporating dose-spacing strategies-may better reflect the current state of knowledge."}
{"hit_count":4439}"}, {"id": "PMID:42911175", "text": "{"id":"PMID:42911175","title":"Risk of Inflammatory Bowel Disease After Anti-CD20 Treatment in Multiple Sclerosis.","year":"2026","journal":"JAMA neurology","abstract":"Importance Anti-CD20 biologics are widely used as disease-modifying therapy (DMT) for relapsing-remitting multiple sclerosis (RRMS), but recent reports have raised concerns about an increased risk of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) during treatment. This study aimed to assess treatment-associated IBD risk in individuals with RRMS. Objective To compare the risk of new-onset IBD across different therapies for RRMS and relative to the general population. Design, setting, and participants This was a population-based cohort study conducted in Sweden. Through the Swedish MS register, initiations of rituximab, natalizumab, dimethyl fumarate, and fingolimod were identified between January 1, 2011, and December 31, 2023, in individuals with RRMS aged 18 to 55 years with at least 10 years of lookback time (5 years for dispensed drugs) and no evidence of previous IBD. These initiations were matched with general population comparator episodes selected from the Swedish population. Exposures Rituximab, comparator DMTs (natalizumab, dimethyl fumarate, fingolimod), or general population comparator. Main outcomes and measures New-onset IBD, defined as 2 or more records on separate dates with the…"}
